\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Pseudo Potentials
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Jan Reimers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
Sept.
 2026
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Chemistry and bonding in molecules and materials is mostly determined by the valance electrons.
 For a suitable definition of what constitutes valance electrons (or orbitals),
 the remaining core electrons play essentially no role in the bonding.
 On the contrary,
 conventional electron structure calculation methods automatically include the core electrons.
 In fact for heavy elements,
 a calculation spends more time on the core electrons,
 than it does on the valance electrons.
 What if there was a way to take the known core orbitals and charge density from an atom calculation,
 and somehow transfer that info into a molecular or solid material calculation,
 so that the computational resources are more efficiently apportioned to (almost) only the valance electrons?
 Analogous to the complimentary Heisenberg and Schrödinger pictures for time dependence,
 there are complimentary ways or carrying over the core orbitals and charge density from an atom calculation:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Modify the trial wave function so this is always orthogonal to the atomic core orbitals.
 OPW and PAW methods.
 In addition we also want to remove the nodes (oscillations) of the valance orbitals.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Modify the electron-nuclear attraction and e-e repulsion potentials to include screening from the core charge density,
 and also force orthogonality to the atomic core orbitals.
 These have the name pseudo potentials (PPs)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It turns out the the form for the PPs is most easily derived from the OPW wave function.
 This text tries to follow the Richard Martin Book (Electronic Structure,
 Basic Theory and Practical Methods),
 chapter 11.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Orthogonalized Plane Waves (OPW)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In it's original form the OPW basis function looks like:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\psi^{OPW}_{\boldsymbol{q}}\left(\boldsymbol{r}\right)=\left|\boldsymbol{q}^{O}\right\rangle =\left|\boldsymbol{q}\right\rangle -\sum_{j}\left|u_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle u_{j}|\boldsymbol{q}\right\rangle \label{eq:OPW}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\boldsymbol{q}\right\rangle =\frac{1}{\sqrt{V}}e^{i\boldsymbol{q}\cdot\boldsymbol{r}}$
\end_inset

 is a plane wave,
 and 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle u_{j}|\boldsymbol{q}\right\rangle =\frac{1}{\sqrt{V}}\intop d^{3}r\:u_{j}\left(\boldsymbol{r}\right)e^{i\boldsymbol{q}\cdot\boldsymbol{r}}\label{eq:Uq}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

 is like a form factor.
 The functions 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{j}\left(\boldsymbol{r}\right)$
\end_inset

 are centred around each nucleus and are assumed to orthonormal 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle u_{i}|u_{j}\right\rangle =\delta_{ij}$
\end_inset

 and anticipate that they will represent atomic core orbitals.
 If we define core projector 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{P}^{core}=\sum_{j}\left|u_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle u_{j}\right|
\]

\end_inset

then we can rewrite 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:OPW"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 in compact form
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left|\boldsymbol{q}^{O}\right\rangle =\left(1-\hat{P}^{core}\right)\left|\boldsymbol{q}\right\rangle .
\]

\end_inset

As usual 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{P}^{2}_{c}$
\end_inset

 is idempotent
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(\hat{P}^{core}\right)^{2}=\sum_{ij}\left|u_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle u_{j}|u_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle u_{j}\right|=\sum_{ij}\left|u_{j}\right\rangle \delta_{ij}\left\langle u_{j}\right|=\hat{P}^{core}
\]

\end_inset

This construction ensures 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi^{OPW}$
\end_inset

 is orthogonal to all 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{j}\left(\boldsymbol{r}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 but it is not necessarily normalized.
 Let us check:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left\langle u_{i}|\boldsymbol{q}^{O}\right\rangle =\left\langle u_{i}|\boldsymbol{q}\right\rangle -\sum_{j}\left\langle u_{i}|u_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle u_{j}|\boldsymbol{q}\right\rangle 
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
=\left\langle u_{i}|\boldsymbol{q}\right\rangle -\sum_{j}\left\langle u_{j}|\boldsymbol{q}\right\rangle \delta_{ij}=0
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left\langle \boldsymbol{q}^{O}|\boldsymbol{q}^{O}\right\rangle =\left\langle \boldsymbol{q}\right|\left(1-\hat{P}_{c}\right)\left(1-\hat{P}_{c}\right)\left|\boldsymbol{q}\right\rangle 
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
=\left\langle \boldsymbol{q}\right|\left[1-2\hat{P}^{core}+\left(\hat{P}^{core}\right)^{2}\right]\left|\boldsymbol{q}\right\rangle =\left\langle \boldsymbol{q}\right|\left(1-\hat{P}^{core}\right)\left|\boldsymbol{q}\right\rangle 
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
=\left(1-\left\langle \boldsymbol{q}\right|\hat{P}^{core}\left|\boldsymbol{q}\right\rangle \right)=1-\sum_{j}\left\langle \boldsymbol{q}|u_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle u_{j}|\boldsymbol{q}\right\rangle 
\]

\end_inset

This is the same as Martin (eq.
 11.10)
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left\langle \psi^{OPW}_{\boldsymbol{q}}|\psi^{OPW}_{\boldsymbol{q}}\right\rangle =1-\sum_{j}\left|\left\langle u_{j}|\boldsymbol{q}\right\rangle \right|^{2}.
\]

\end_inset

The sum index 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is doing a lot of heavy lifting here and warrants some clarification.
 We can expand the label 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 to include the atom index 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

,
 and additionally the 
\begin_inset Formula $n,l,m$
\end_inset

 quantum numbers of the core orbitals on atom 
\begin_inset Formula $a.$
\end_inset

 Or 
\begin_inset Formula $j=\left\{ a,n,l,m\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And this immediately raises the question:
 How can we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle u_{i}|u_{j}\right\rangle =\delta_{ij}$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 correspond to two different atoms?
 Also if the 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|u_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 are identified with atomic orbitals then they should satisfy
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{H}\left|u_{j}\right\rangle =\varepsilon_{j}\left|u_{j}\right\rangle \label{eq:ApproxAtomEigen}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

but only approximately.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}=-\frac{1}{2}\nabla^{2}+\sum_{a}\hat{V}_{a}$
\end_inset

 where the potential portion is a sum over atoms.
 If we expand 
\begin_inset Formula $j=\left\{ a,n,l,m\right\} $
\end_inset

 then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left[-\frac{1}{2}\nabla^{2}+\hat{V}_{a}\right]\left|u_{a,nlm}\right\rangle =\varepsilon_{a,nlm}\left|u_{a,nlm}\right\rangle \label{eq:TrueAtomEigen}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

but
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{V}_{b}\left|u_{a,nlm}\right\rangle \neq0,\;b\neq a.
\]

\end_inset

But by the the definition of a core orbital,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|u_{a,nlm}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 is exponentially small at the other atom centres.
 This just a modified version of our original hypothesis:
 
\emph on
core electrons play essentially no role in the bonding
\emph default
.
 No role in bonding means negligible overlap with neighbour atom orbitals.
 But that also means negligible coulomb attraction with neighbour nuclei
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left\langle u_{a,nlm}\right|\frac{1}{\left|\boldsymbol{R}_{b}-\boldsymbol{r}\right|}\left|u_{a,nlm}\right\rangle \approx0,\;a\neq b
\]

\end_inset

In addition these error from the environment will slightly shift the eigen values,
 but barely changes the shape of core orbitals.
 If you ever find that the environment error is too large then your selection of valance electrons is unsuitable.
 You will need to add the next deeper shell into the valance category.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can still use projectors when 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle u_{i}|u_{j}\right\rangle =S_{ij}\neq\delta_{ij}$
\end_inset

.
 We simply generalize the definition:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{P}^{core}=\sum_{j}\left|u_{j}\right\rangle \left(S^{-1}_{ij}\right)\left\langle u_{j}\right|.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Projector on a Lattice
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{P}^{core}$
\end_inset

 commutes with lattice translations,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\hat{T}_{\mathbf{R}}\hat{P}^{core}\hat{T}{}^{\dagger}_{\mathbf{R}}=\hat{P}^{core}}$
\end_inset

,
 because translating relabels the sum over sites.
 Also 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\hat{T}_{\mathbf{R}}|\mathbf{q}\rangle=e^{-i\mathbf{q}\cdot\mathbf{R}}|\mathbf{q}\rangle}$
\end_inset

,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\hat{T}_{\mathbf{R}}|\mathbf{q}^{O}\rangle=e^{-i\mathbf{q}\cdot\mathbf{R}}|\mathbf{q}^{O}\rangle}$
\end_inset

:
 the OPW is a Bloch function of wave-vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{q}$
\end_inset

 so irrep blocking of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

still works.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The valance orbital function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Any orbital eigen function build up from OPW basis functions should automatically avoid variational collapse into core states.
 In addition we are going to postulate a smooth,
 node-less function for the valance orbitals:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle =\sum_{\boldsymbol{q}}c_{i\boldsymbol{q}}\left|\boldsymbol{q}\right\rangle .\label{eq:psi-smooth}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The valance orbital function is then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left|\psi^{v}_{i}\right\rangle =\left(1-\hat{P}_{c}\right)\left|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle =\left|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle -\sum_{\boldsymbol{q}}c_{i\boldsymbol{q}}\sum_{j}\left|u_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle u_{j}|\boldsymbol{q}\right\rangle 
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
=\left|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle +\sum_{j}B_{ij}\left|u_{j}\right\rangle \label{eq:psi-valance}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
B_{ij}=-\sum_{\boldsymbol{q}}c_{i\boldsymbol{q}}\left\langle u_{j}|\boldsymbol{q}\right\rangle =-\left\langle u_{j}|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle 
\]

\end_inset

We don't yet know what the eigen equation is for this node-less function,
 but we do know that eigen equation for the real,
 node-full eigen function:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{H}\left|\psi^{v}_{i}\right\rangle =\left[-\frac{1}{2}\nabla^{2}+V\left(\boldsymbol{r}\right)\right]\left|\psi^{v}_{i}\right\rangle =\varepsilon_{i}\left|\psi^{v}_{i}\right\rangle .\label{eq:psiv-eigen}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Pseudo Potentials
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The PKA (Phillips Kleinman Antoncik) approach is to substitute 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:psi-valance"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 into the valance orbital eigen equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:psiv-eigen"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 and isolate and corresponding orbital eigen equation for the smooth part 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\left(\boldsymbol{r}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}\left(1-\hat{P}_{c}\right)\left|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle =\varepsilon_{i}\left(1-\hat{P}_{c}\right)\left|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle 
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}\left|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle -\sum_{j}\hat{H}\left|u_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle u_{j}|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle +\varepsilon_{i}\sum_{j}\left|u_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle u_{j}|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle =\varepsilon_{i}\left|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle 
\]

\end_inset

If we assume 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:ApproxAtomEigen"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 then we can reduce the second term
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}\left|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle -\sum_{j}\varepsilon_{j}\left|u_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle u_{j}|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle +\varepsilon_{i}\sum_{j}\left|u_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle u_{j}|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle =\varepsilon_{i}\left|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle 
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}\left|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle +\sum_{j}\left(\varepsilon_{i}-\varepsilon_{j}\right)\left|u_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle u_{j}|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle =\varepsilon_{i}\left|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle 
\]

\end_inset

or
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[-\frac{1}{2}\nabla^{2}+V\left(\boldsymbol{r}\right)+\hat{V}^{R}\right]\left|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle =\varepsilon_{i}\left|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle 
\]

\end_inset

The two potential terms is our desired PKA version of a PP
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{V}^{PP}=V\left(\boldsymbol{r}\right)+\hat{V}^{R}=V\left(\boldsymbol{r}\right)+\sum_{j}\left(\varepsilon_{i}-\varepsilon_{j}\right)\left|u_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle u_{j}\right|.
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\hat{V}^{R}$
\end_inset

 is essentially what Martin shows in eq.
 11.14.
 At this point it is not clear that 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 is actually node-less.
 But the new (repulsive because 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon_{i}>\varepsilon_{j}$
\end_inset

) 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{V}^{R}$
\end_inset

 prevents variation collapse of 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 into atomic core states,
 and also shields most of strong core attraction in 
\begin_inset Formula $V\left(\boldsymbol{r}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Non-uniqueness
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 is not unique.
 We can see this by adding a certain term to 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 as follows
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left|\psi^{v}_{i}\right\rangle =\left(1-\hat{P}_{c}\right)\left(\left|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle +\sum_{k}a_{k}\left|u_{k}\right\rangle \right)
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(1-\hat{P}_{c}\right)\left|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle +\sum_{k}a_{k}\left|u_{k}\right\rangle -\sum_{j}\left|u_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle u_{j}\right|\sum_{k}a_{k}\left|u_{k}\right\rangle 
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(1-\hat{P}_{c}\right)\left|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle +\sum_{k}a_{k}\left|u_{k}\right\rangle -\sum_{j}\left|u_{j}\right\rangle \sum_{k}a_{k}\delta_{kj}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
=\left(1-\hat{P}_{c}\right)\left|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle 
\]

\end_inset

So we have a huge amount of gauge freedom in 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\tilde{\psi}^{v}_{i}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 which can be used (by carefully choosing the 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{k}$
\end_inset

 numbers) to get ride of those pesky oscillating nodes and make it smooth.
\end_layout

